[#:seon.task{:name "each-commit-forces-the-directory-once-per-node"
             :intent "FIXED ON THE KONSERVE FORK 08465d6 (reviewed; unpushed; adoption = push, apply tmp/commit-floor-research-opus/patches/adopt-konserve-deferred-sync.patch, restart; the store opts in with :defer-immutable-sync?; new immutable keys defer, existing keys and the head pay two directory full syncs): the JDK's FileChannel.force is F_FULLFSYNC on macOS (3.78 ms against 0.03 ms for FileDescriptor.sync); a settlement commit wrote 14.8 keys x 2 forces = 29.6 drive flushes, 71-76 ms of I/O; scratch replay: new keys 12-15 ms. Original: One small commit on default writes 4-8 index nodes (~120-175 KB each) concurrently and Konserve forces the store directory after each, then the commit record and the branch head each force blob and directory in order: 12-16 forces, device-serialized at ~4 ms each, ~45-50 ms of a 64 ms commit. The nodes share one directory, so one directory force after all node blobs are forced and renamed keeps durability and order (the record still lands after it): measured 38.4 -> 32 ms per commit shape (6 x 150 KB nodes + two ordered records). Needs a Konserve/Datahike fork change (write-pending-kvs!), not a config setting; the per-key path was ruled 2026-09-23, so bring it to the owner with this measurement. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration`, `docs/research/agent-platform/commit-floor-2026-09-26.md`, `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-eval-overhead-2026-09-26.md` and `docs/seon/issues/file-store-commits-pay-five-times-the-fsyncs-they-need.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-small-commit-rewrites-thirty-index-nodes"
             :intent "At 128/32 (4d1f147a7) a 19-37 datom run on default writes 30-41 index nodes (180-260 KB, 235-336 KB on 4 KiB blocks) plus the 88 KB fused-root record, twice (commit record and branch head): 420-520 KB per commit, one commit per eval_clj run (2026-09-28 live attribution, tmp/store-writes/evidence.edn). AEVT and temporal AEVT are 55-65% of the nodes: every distinct attribute a commit touches is its own AEVT leaf, and the diff buffer does not absorb them. LZ4 store values (0241f02c9) halve bytes on the throwaway replay; remaining levers ranked in the evidence file: the head written as a second full record (each-commit-serializes-its-root-record-twice), the 4 KiB block floor (~25% of node disk; a packing backend trades it for compaction write amplification), temporal indexes (~40% of node bytes; keep-history is the as-of capability). Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-writes--evidence.edn` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-writes--lz4_proof.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "index-roots-lose-their-subtree-counts"
             :intent "Every boot logs datahike.query.estimate 'Index lacks precomputed subtree counts (old database format)': on default (created at 128/32) the EAVT, AEVT and all three temporal roots hold subtree count -1; AVET keeps 233,196. Not a creation flag, not keep-history, not our Datahike fork: persistent-sorted-set 0.4.137 Branch.java sets a parent's count to -1 whenever a mutated child's count is unknown, serializes the -1, and nothing recomputes it (a fresh store has counts on all six; 50 upserts on a throwaway store lost AVET's at diff-buf 0, tmp/store-writes/subtree_counts.clj). The planner then uses heuristic cardinalities. Counts cost one long per branch node (negligible bytes). A nuke restores them only until ordinary writes decay them again; the fix is in PSS: apply the add/remove delta (+1/-1) to a known parent count without requiring the child's count, then measure planner choices on default's slow queries. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/growth/growth-notes.md`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-writes--evidence.edn` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-writes--subtree_counts.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-commit-serializes-its-root-record-twice"
             :intent "Datahike's commit! writes the same db-to-store value (the fused-root record, about 134 KB on default) twice, as the immutable commit record and as the mutable branch head, and konserve serializes each write separately: two sequential serializations on every commit's critical path (about 5-8 ms of the 26 ms of CPU a settlement commit spends outside I/O). One serialization written under both keys, or a head that names the durable commit record, removes one; either is a Datahike fork change. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration` and `docs/research/agent-platform/commit-floor-2026-09-26.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-ordinary-commit-writes-26-files-and-136-kb"
             :intent "Measured on live default (4b29a0689, 2026-09-28): a (+ 1 2) eval is one commit of 30 datoms over 19 distinct attributes and writes 23-28 files / 120-151 KB; a defn plus call writes 61-126 files / 394-801 KB (64 datoms over 44 attributes, about 6 KB per datom). One (+ 1 2) commit: commit record 21.7 KB and branch head 21.6 KB (16% each; :fuse-index-roots? inlines all six roots, written twice, writing.cljc:143-193/523-528), aevt nodes 63.7 KB in 15 files (47%), avet 17 KB (13%), eavt 11.5 KB (8%). The floor is O(distinct attributes touched x branching factor x slot bytes) plus two heads, not the datoms; the aevt/avet share follows from 19-44 one-datom attributes per eval. Done when the levers ranked in the evidence section 4 are tried on a branch of the live store and a (+ 1 2) commit is timed and byte-counted before and after (unfused or single-written heads, fewer distinct attributes per eval settlement, aevt/avet node packing), with the store-writes tasks it builds on (a-small-commit-rewrites-thirty-index-nodes, each-commit-serializes-its-root-record-twice) closed or restated by the result. Also: per the evidence the store holds 346,605 files / 1.07 GB (98% index nodes, 43% of du bytes are 4 KB block rounding), collection has never run, and 412-426 dead branch heads (rehearsal 197, building-source 143, checkpoint 40) sit outside the roster. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/commit-floor--report.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-growth--report.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "collection-lists-the-store-keys-twice"
             :intent "Konserve patches to drop the second k/keys listing in collection (astra-referenced-blobs), which lists 346k keys per pass. Done when collection lists once, timed on the live store size. Read `seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/store-growth--report.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-commit-serializes-hundreds-of-times-the-bytes-it-writes"
             :intent "One commit's durable write allocates 70-100 times the bytes it stores: a defn pass writes 132-161 index nodes (590-690 KB) and allocates about 70 MB on the writer threads; an ordinary pass writes 36-44 nodes (270-290 KB) for about 18 MB (defn-why, 2026-10-08, JFR on default). The owners are dependency code: fressian's WriteHandlerLookup resolving a handler per written object through Fns.soloEntry, which allocates a HashMap entry iterator each time (13 MB of a defn's 71 MB), FressianWriter.doWrite (20 MB), a new LZ4FrameOutputStream with fresh 64 KB block buffers per node (konserve compressor, 10 MB), and konserve's io state machines. A plain Datahike file store with history at 480,000 datoms, outside Seon, allocates 20 MB for a 27-datom transaction and 39 MB for 60 datoms. Node count per commit is task each-commit-writes-dozens-of-index-nodes territory; this task is bytes allocated per byte written. Fix in the forks: cache write handlers by class, reuse LZ4 buffers per writer thread. Done when allocation per commit is within 10 times the bytes written. Read `seon.db/transact!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/defn-why/defn-why.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-world-rereads-index-nodes-from-disk"
             :intent "A check whose test body runs one Datahike query on its fresh test world reads index nodes from disk again. (`my.test/check` {:seon.test/changed ['seon.task/step-summary]}) on a throwaway root at f4008d441 took 11.3 s and allocated 4.9 GB JVM-wide (reply :seon.repl/alloc-mb 2554; merge-path-opus measured 2.74 GB for a two-test check on default). JFR allocation samples put 2.41 GB in one form: the contracts query of `seon.render.schema/usage` (`[?arity ?attribute ?k] [?f :seon.fn/arities ?arity]`, :seon.fn/arities an unindexed component ref), called from `seon.render.ns-test/task-and-schema-entities-render-as-graph-nodes` inside `seon.test-support/with-database`. Datahike's fused scan probes EAVT once per scanned datom (`datahike.query.execute/execute-fused-scan-rel`, pss-lookup-ge), and each probe misses the node cache and restores the node from konserve (persistent_set CachedStorage restore: LZ4FrameInputStream.readHeader 537 MB, UnixPath 325 MB). On the cluster's own warm connection the same query costs 26-330 ms and 50-485 MB. Suspected owner, not yet proven: every test request opens a new overlay store (`seon.cluster.store/open-overlay!`, a new d/connect), so its node cache starts empty. Done when a test world reads the shared store's index nodes through a cache it shares with the cluster's connection (or the measured cause is fixed at its own owner), no konserve node is read twice in one request, and this check allocates under 300 MB, with a regression counting storage reads through seon.mcp/call. Read `seon.test/clean-request-handle` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/merge-path/merge-path-opus.md`."}
